## Consensus findings (majority of panel agrees)
- **[low] pipeline/post_live_prefetch.py:29** The live_status() yt-dlp probe (timeout=90 s) runs AFTER _download_window has already exhausted its 120 s section-download timeout, extending the worst-case per-video wall time in the lock pre-check from 120 s to 210 s (a 75 % increase). For a pin with N YouTube videos the pre-check can now take up to 210 N seconds instead of 120 N seconds before the GPU lease is granted. It is bounded and does not crash, but it lengthens the pre-check window.
  - evidence: `r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=90)`
  - fix: Reduce the live_status probe timeout to 30 s (a --skip-download -J probe on a reachable video returns in <5 s; 30 s is generous for a slow network) or cache the live_status from the _download_window yt-dlp invocation if it is exposed, to avoid a second full yt-dlp startup.
  - raised by qwen27b:F1; agree: qwen27b
- **[low] pipeline/run_fresh.py:481** Segments that lack a 'source' key are silently classified as YouTube: (s.get("source") or {}).get("kind", "youtube") defaults to "youtube". If the pin format ever contains a non-YouTube segment without an explicit source field, it will be passed to post_live_prefetch.prefetch (and thus to yt-dlp) as if it were a YouTube video_id, producing a spurious yt-dlp call and a confusing log line rather than a clean skip.
  - evidence: `segs = [s for s in json.load(f)["segments"] if (s.get("source") or {}).get("kind", "youtube") == "youtube"]`
  - fix: Change the default to a sentinel that is never a valid kind, e.g. .get("kind", "") == "youtube", so only segments that explicitly declare kind=="youtube" (or have no source at all AND the pipeline guarantees all source-less segments are YouTube) are included. If the data model guarantees source-less segments are always YouTube, add a comment documenting that invariant.
  - raised by qwen27b:F2; agree: qwen27b

